WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.429
Welcome to Module Five: Troubleshoot and Optimize.

2
00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:06.688
It's about finding what's broken in a GPU server,

3
00:00:06.738 --> 00:00:09.789
fixing it cleanly, and making healthy servers fast.

4
00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:13.717
It's three a.m., and an on-call engineer is paged again.

5
00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:17.859
Training jobs keep dying, and every one of them ran on the same node.

6
00:00:18.659 --> 00:00:22.854
The tempting move is to guess: reboot the node, resubmit the job, and hope.

7
00:00:23.184 --> 00:00:26.334
Hours later the job dies again, and nobody knows why.

8
00:00:27.134 --> 00:00:31.315
So she starts with evidence: the kernel log shows Xid seventy-nine,

9
00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:33.463
a GPU that has fallen off the bus.

10
00:00:33.793 --> 00:00:38.628
Running nvidia-smi confirms it, listing seven GPUs instead of eight.

11
00:00:39.428 --> 00:00:43.943
Next she checks the BMC event log, which records the server's own sensors.

12
00:00:44.273 --> 00:00:45.860
A fan failed hours earlier,

13
00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:49.557
and the GPUs have been overheating and throttling ever since.

14
00:00:50.357 --> 00:00:52.374
Now she follows the disciplined path:

15
00:00:52.424 --> 00:00:55.448
drain the node, so the scheduler stops sending it work.

16
00:00:55.778 --> 00:00:58.821
Then collect a bug report, before touching any hardware.

17
00:00:59.621 --> 00:01:04.435
The fan is swapped, and the damaged GPU goes back to the vendor under an RMA.

18
00:01:04.765 --> 00:01:08.918
Only after diagnostics pass again does the node return to service.

19
00:01:09.718 --> 00:01:13.081
A second node looks perfectly healthy, with no errors anywhere.

20
00:01:13.411 --> 00:01:17.116
Yet every job on it runs noticeably slower than on its neighbours.

21
00:01:17.916 --> 00:01:21.617
The culprit is the BIOS: it was left in a power-saving profile,

22
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.285
with the wrong NUMA setting.

23
00:01:23.615 --> 00:01:26.231
Fix both, and the node keeps pace with its peers.

24
00:01:27.031 --> 00:01:32.592
Lesson one teaches you to identify faulty GPUs, fans, NICs, and power supplies.

25
00:01:32.922 --> 00:01:36.476
You'll read Xid errors, and use nvidia-smi,

26
00:01:36.526 --> 00:01:39.912
DCGM diagnostics, and NVSM health checks.

27
00:01:40.712 --> 00:01:43.068
You'll learn which Xid codes point to hardware,

28
00:01:43.118 --> 00:01:45.270
and which usually point to the application.

29
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.829
And the BMC event log catches what the operating system can't see,

30
00:01:49.879 --> 00:01:52.163
like failed fans and power supplies.

31
00:01:52.963 --> 00:01:57.521
Lesson two covers replacing faulty cards, GPUs, and power supplies.

32
00:01:57.851 --> 00:02:00.415
The order matters: drain, collect logs,

33
00:02:00.465 --> 00:02:03.967
open an RMA, swap the part, re-validate, then resume.

34
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:09.474
Lesson three optimises AMD and Intel servers, starting in the BIOS.

35
00:02:09.804 --> 00:02:13.588
You'll set the power profile, choose the NUMA layout,

36
00:02:13.638 --> 00:02:16.047
and check PCIe and IOMMU settings.

37
00:02:16.847 --> 00:02:21.085
Then you'll pair each GPU with the NIC and CPU socket closest to it.

38
00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:25.824
And you'll tune storage, so data arrives fast enough to keep the GPUs busy.

39
00:02:26.624 --> 00:02:29.110
This domain is twelve percent of the exam,

40
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.974
mostly scenarios asking what to check first, or what to do next.

41
00:02:33.304 --> 00:02:35.750
Lesson one starts with reading the evidence.
